                <pb facs="0628" n="528" sameAs="frus:frus1884/pg528"/>

                <head>No. 406.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Thomas to Mr.
                    Frelinghuysen.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Stockholm</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1884-05-03"><hi rendition="#italic">May</hi> 3, 1884</date>.
                        (Received May 21.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 65.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to my dispatch No. 29 on the
                    subject of direct steam communication between the United States and Sweden, I
                    have the honor to inform you that the Leipzig, the pioneer steamer of the first
                    direct steamship line between the two countries, sailed from Gothenburg, Sweden,
                    direct for New York, at 2 o’clock on the afternoon of May 1, having on board 228
                    emigrants, and 220 tons cargo.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>W. W. THOMAS, <hi rendition="#small-caps">Jr.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
